  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 257 AM CDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nThe pattern begins to change late this week. A mid-level longwave \ntrough circling over the Four Corners area is expected to split, \nwith its poleward section phasing into another trough traversing \nwestern Canada. This shift will pull a cold front into the Upper \nMidwest Saturday and orient it zonally on Sunday as the shortwave \nzips into eastern Canada. The Mid-Mississippi Valley will remain \nwell south of the cold front over the weekend, and our temperature \ntrends will reflect that. LREF probabilities of 1pm temperatures >69 \ndegrees Saturday and Sunday both range from 75-100%, and the NBM max \ntemperature spread for each day is only 3 degrees. The cold front is \nexpected to finally push into the region on Tuesday, but cooler \nweather is not guaranteed especially for locations south of I-70. \nGuidance suggests that the front may be weakening as it arrives, and \nthat it may become stationary before it clears the area. This would \nmean that less cool air would filter into less of the CWA and that \nthe warm streak may continue through Tuesday. Confidence in this, or \nfor any solution, is still very low given the timeframe in question. \nAgain, the NBM max temperature spread reflects this well as it \nclimbs to 10+ degrees on Tuesday and nearly 20 degrees on\nWednesday.",
